      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:09 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nSHAY DVORETZKY, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nJONATHAN Y. ELLIS, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Shay Dvoretzky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If this case involved a domestic arbitration agreement, GE Energy could enforce it as long as it could satisfy domestic non-signatory enforcement doctrines like equitable estoppel. The question here is whether the New York Convention prohibits that same result for international arbitration agreements. It does not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Shareholders shall appoint two directors to the board. Does that mean shareholders can appoint 20 directors to the board?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is it -- is it necessary to go so far as to say that the Convention says nothing about what the relevant law of a particular jurisdiction says about who can enforce an arbitration agreement or could it say -- could it perhaps go beyond strictly the signatories to the agreement and encompass some other non-parties that have a sufficient -- that have a close connection, as would be the case with somebody who was equitably estopped?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jonathan Y. Ellis",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The New York Convention place an important but limited role in the recognition of international arbitration agreements. It requires contracting states to recognize and enforce those agreements in certain circumstances, but it does not, as my friend says, establish a comprehensive set of rules for arbitration."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Excuse me -- MR. ELLIS: -- compelling arbitration on estoppel grounds. JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: -- there are two ways to reach your result. One is to read Article II and say what you seem to be saying, which is that it only requires or compels arbitration in one circumstance but a contracting state can compel arbitration in any way that it wants, even without a written agreement. That seems to be the essence of your argument, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. ELLIS",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ELLIS: Yes. JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: That's odd, indeed, because as Justice -- as the Chief Justice noted and Justice Kagan noted, it seems always that a signed written agreement respecting consent is a minimum requirement. Or another way to get to where you want to go, another reading, is that Article II does not allow contracting states to compel arbitration whenever it wants, even without a written agreement but that they can compel it if someone is a party, that that's undefined."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Counsel, I'm -- I understand that different countries may have different views about equitable estoppel or other kinds of non-signatory, non-strict consent arbitrations. Is there any disagreement among countries about how to read the Convention itself with respect to whether it creates a floor or a ceiling?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. HACKER",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "GE cannot compel Outokumpu to arbitrate its tort claim with GE because there is no written arbitration agreement between them. I agree that would generally not be an obstacle in a domestic arbitration case because, as this Court held in Arthur Andersen, Chapter 1's agreement enforcement provision, FAA Section 3, does not limit enforcement to \"parties to a written agreement.\" But the lack of a written agreement is decisive here because the Convention's enforcement provision, Article II, Section 3, is limited to the parties to a written arbitration agreement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What do we have to decide? The -- the Eleventh Circuit said a non-signatory can never enforce, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: So where do you get that understanding from?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Can you tell us what is --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I'm interested in -- you want to read that sentence 3 as the ceiling. You know what I am talking about?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Shay Dvoretzky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, there's an international consensus in favor of non-signatory enforcement generally. And there are numerous international cases that allow non-signatory enforcement on facts like these. The Titan Unity case from Singapore, there are cases from France and Switzerland, all of these are very similar. You have a situation where A contracts with B and C actually performs the contract. And in those situations because C is involved in performing A and B's contract, C can enforce the arbitration agreement if sued by one of the parties to the contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What are they?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Shay Dvoretzky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Hacker argues that Congress adopted the Convention as both a floor and a ceiling for U.S. law. That's simply not what Congress did in Chapter 2."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Shay Dvoretzky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the narrowest possible way, is to simply hold that the Eleventh Circuit was wrong to -- to apply a signatory requirement, at Petition Appendix 15A to 16A, the Eleventh Circuit recounts the district court's finding that we were parties but says the reason we can't enforce is that we didn't actually sign. I think that's demonstrably wrong and the narrowest possible way is to send it back for that reason."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The second?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1048",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2cdcb1d2c9a250bae91d9af2d27232adfacbfab1f28a0d4706de6be3a98d360e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2cdcb1d2c9a2-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted. (Whereupon, at 12:10 p.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
